Two more valid Codex findings on top of a19089e.

P1, compact strip pane resolution: the compact strip renders EVERY pane's tabs
but carries one data-workspace-pane-id, set to panes[last]. Resolving a drop
from that id sent a tab released over a non-final pane's tab into the last
pane, and counted its insertion index across all panes' tabs. Now a drop onto a
tab belongs to whichever pane owns that tab (paneIdForTab), and the index
counts only that pane's neighbours; the strip's own id is used only for
trailing space, which is the one case it actually describes.

Regression test proven to catch it, not just to pass: temporarily reverted the
pane resolution and the new test failed with Settings landing at the END
(dumped into the last pane), then passed once restored. Always check a new
regression test fails without its fix - three of the tests in this PR would
otherwise have been vacuous.

P2, literal NUL bytes: movedToPane compared orders with ids.join('\0') and I
had written actual 0x00 bytes into the source. rg reported 'binary file
matches' and suppressed output, hiding WorkspaceShell.svelte from searches.
Now compares element by element. Check with: tr -dc '\000' < file | wc -c.
Worth knowing that an Edit can silently introduce a control character that
typecheck, build and 171 tests all accept.

Frontend CI went green on a19089e, confirming the geometry fix resolved the
original CI-only failure.

